## Key Concepts

### Nix
The package manager and language underlying everything. Nix is:
- **Declarative** — you describe what you want, not the steps to get there
- **Reproducible** — same config always produces the same result
- **Atomic** — changes either fully apply or don't apply at all, no partial states
- **Rollback-capable** — every change creates a new generation; old ones are preserved

### Flakes
The modern way to write Nix configs. A flake is a directory with a `flake.nix` that:
- Declares **inputs** (where to get packages from, e.g. nixpkgs on GitHub)
- Declares **outputs** (what to build, e.g. your home config or NixOS system)
- Creates a `flake.lock` that pins exact versions of all inputs

Flakes are still technically "experimental" but are the standard in practice.
Enabled in `~/.config/nix/nix.conf` with:
```
experimental-features = nix-command flakes
```

### nixpkgs
The giant repository of all Nix packages (~100,000+). You're using `nixos-unstable`
which gets the latest versions. Search packages at: https://search.nixos.org/packages

### home-manager
A tool that manages your user environment using Nix. It can:
- Install packages into your user profile
- Write config files for your tools (neovim, bat, zsh, git, etc.)
- Set environment variables and shell aliases
- Manage dotfiles declaratively

On **Mac**: runs standalone, activated with `home-manager switch`
On **Dell**: runs as a NixOS module, activated as part of `nixos-rebuild switch`

### Generations
Every time you run `home-manager switch` or `nixos-rebuild switch`, Nix creates
a new **generation** — a complete snapshot of your environment. You can always
roll back to a previous generation if something breaks.

```bash
# list home-manager generations
home-manager generations

# list NixOS generations (Dell)
sudo nix-env --list-generations --profile /nix/var/nix/profiles/system
```

