## Nix Language Basics

Nix has its own language for writing configs. These are the patterns you'll
see most often:

```nix
# basic assignment — every line ends with a semicolon
name = "tristan";
age = 30;
enabled = true;

# attribute set (like a dictionary/object)
person = {
  name = "tristan";
  age = 30;
};

# list
packages = [ "bat" "fzf" "btop" ];

# with statement — brings all attributes of a set into scope
# instead of pkgs.bat pkgs.fzf pkgs.btop you can write:
home.packages = with pkgs; [ bat fzf btop ];

# function — Nix configs are mostly functions
{ config, pkgs, ... }:   # arguments
{                         # return value (your config)
  home.username = "tristan";
}

# import — load another file
modules = [ ./home.nix ];   # ./ means relative to current directory

# string interpolation
system = "aarch64-darwin";
url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/${system}";

# comments
# this is a comment
```

### Common Mistakes
```nix
# WRONG — missing semicolon
shell = pkgs.zsh

# RIGHT
shell = pkgs.zsh;

# WRONG — wrong nesting style for git settings
userName = "Tristan";

# RIGHT
user = {
  name = "Tristan";
};

# WRONG — absolute path in flake (forbidden in pure mode)
import /home/tristan/home.nix

# RIGHT — relative path
import ./home.nix
```

